  "data_url": "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/pride/archive/projects/PXD077321",
  "verified": {
    "opened_data_files": true,
    "what_i_actually_checked": "Downloaded and parsed PRIDE PXD077321 Pv_ID.csv (453 rows x 21 columns, CC0) and the four preprint supplementary spreadsheets (Research Square rs-6682089 v1): the peptide table (453 Plasmodium vivax rows / 446 unique sequences; 3,357 human rows / 3,191 unique; seven per-donor sheets summing to 1,025 peptide-spectrum matches; 166-protein frequency sheet), the 50 synthesised peptides sheet, the PlasmoDB expression sheets (~170 genes per species), and the patient clinical sheet. I computed peptide length distributions, score distributions and duplicate counts myself. I also listed every PRIDE file (15 files, 14 raw .raw totalling 6.28 GB plus the one search-result CSV).",
    "what_came_from_paper_text_only": "From the paper PDF text only: that the seven donors were HLA-typed (reported in Extended Data Fig. 4a, a figure, not a table); that NetMHCpan 4.0 at a 10% rank threshold was used to call 23 of 50 peptides 'binders'; that interferon-gamma ELISpot responses were measured in Plasmodium vivax-infected, Plasmodium falciparum-infected and healthy donors; that non-human-primate and rodent immunisation experiments were done. None of those response values appear in any table I could open. Springer's supplementary-file server returned HTTP 403, so I could not open the published version's fifth supplement ('HLA binding')."
  },
  "part_a_killers": {
    "a1_downloadable": {"pass": true, "note": "PRIDE PXD077321 is public under CC0 and I downloaded the 118 KB Pv_ID.csv directly; the four preprint supplementary spreadsheets also downloaded. Only the published Nature supplement server (static-content.springer.com) returned 403."},
    "a2_label_is_a_column": {"pass": false, "note": "No label column exists in any file. Three candidate labels all have to be built: (i) 'presented on HLA class I, yes/no' exists only as a list of positives, so every negative must be invented from the proteome; (ii) 'recognised by T cells', the interesting label, is interferon-gamma ELISpot spot counts that appear only inside figure panels, never in a table; (iii) 'which HLA allele presents this peptide' would need motif deconvolution because each donor carries up to six class I alleles."},
    "a3_inputs_present": {"pass": true, "note": "Peptide amino-acid sequences are present in full (with modification annotations in parentheses), plus UniProt accessions for the source parasite proteins, so no identifier lookups are needed for the input side."},
    "a4_units_stated": {"pass": true, "note": "Columns carry explicit units: mass in daltons, m/z, retention time in minutes, mass error in ppm, PEAKS confidence as -10*log10(p), expression as TPM (transcripts per million). Checked in the files."},
    "a5_enough_rows": {"pass": false, "n_rows": 446, "note": "446 unique Plasmodium peptides and 3,191 unique human peptides, but from only 7 donors, and all are positives. The one measured, biologically meaningful label (T cell recognition by ELISpot) covers 50 peptides. The generalisation unit is the donor (n=7) or the HLA allele (typed only inside a figure), not the peptide."},
    "a6_fits_laptop": {"pass": true, "working_set": "~0.9 MB of spreadsheets plus a 118 KB CSV", "note": "Everything usable is tiny. The 6.28 GB of raw mass-spectrometry .raw files is both over the project's 50 MB cap and excluded by the no-raw-data rule, but is not needed."},
    "a7_counts_match_paper": {"pass": true, "note": "Verified: the file has exactly 453 Plasmodium peptide rows and 166 source proteins, matching the abstract's '453 unique peptides, mapping to 166 proteins'. Minor discrepancy I found myself: 453 rows contain only 446 distinct sequences, so 'unique peptides' counts rows not sequences."},
    "a8_label_provenance": {"pass": false, "note": "The only binary label the paper actually writes down, 'binder vs non-binder' for the 50 tested peptides, is NetMHCpan 4.0 output, i.e. another model's prediction, so training on it would just distil NetMHCpan. The presentation label itself is a PEAKS 7 database-search call at a permissive -10*log10(p) >= 15 cutoff with no false-discovery rate stated; 43% of rows sit below 20, top human hits are keratin contaminants, and the length distribution is flat from 8 to 15 residues (only 15% nine-mers) rather than the strong nine-mer peak a genuine HLA class I immunopeptidome shows, so a large fraction of these 'presented' peptides are likely co-purified degradation products."
  },
  "part_a_verdict": "reject",
