  "backbone": {"name": "ESM-2 (protein language model, small CPU-runnable checkpoints) for peptide sequences", "freeze_and_head_viable": true, "hardware": "Laptop CPU; 446 short peptides embed in under a minute", "licence": "MIT, permissive"},
  "integration": {"partner_dataset": "IEDB (Immune Epitope Database) MHC ligand and T cell assay records, plus UniProt/PlasmoDB for the parasite proteome", "join_key": "Peptide amino-acid sequence, and UniProt accession for the source protein", "bigger_question_one_sentence": "Do HLA class I presentation predictors trained almost entirely on human and tumour peptides transfer to peptides from an intracellular parasite displayed on infected human reticulocytes? — a good question that this dataset unfortunately cannot answer, because its own human peptide set has a non-HLA-like length distribution and so cannot serve as the internal positive control the comparison requires."},
  "controls": {"positive_control": "The 3,357 human peptides eluted from the same pulldowns should show the textbook nine-mer-dominated HLA class I length profile. I checked this and it fails: nine-mers are 14% of human peptides and lengths run to 25 residues, so the internal positive control does not pass.", "negative_control": "Label shuffling would be runnable, but there is no label to shuffle.", "external_validation": "IEDB holds roughly 1,976 Plasmodium MHC ligand assay entries and ~6,874 experimentally validated Plasmodium epitopes, which would be a plausible external set — but joining to it does not repair the missing label column here."},
  "honest_claim_h3": "Any model built on this would claim only: 'among peptides that a single PEAKS 7 search at a permissive score cutoff assigned to Plasmodium vivax in anti-HLA pulldowns from seven Brazilian donors, these sequence features separate them from random proteome peptides' — which is a statement about a search-engine artefact as much as about antigen presentation.",
  "plain_idea": {
    "one_liner": "Use parasite peptide sequence to predict which malaria peptides human immune-presentation molecules display and which ones T cells attack.",
    "train_on": "The candidate inputs are 446 unique Plasmodium vivax peptide sequences (8-15 amino acids) eluted from HLA class I molecules on infected red-cell precursors from 7 Brazilian patients, plus 3,191 human peptides from the same pulldowns, plus per-protein expression across parasite life-cycle stages for ~170 genes.",
    "predict": "Either (a) whether a peptide is displayed on HLA class I, measured by mass spectrometry after antibody pulldown, or (b) whether it triggers an interferon-gamma T cell response, measured by ELISpot — but (b) was done for only 50 peptides and the counts appear only in figures.",
    "why_worthwhile": "Malaria vaccine developers need to know which parasite peptides human killer T cells actually see; a transferable predictor would let them screen whole Plasmodium proteomes instead of running immunopeptidomics on scarce patient blood."
  },
  "paper_type": "experimental",
  "crowded_field_case": {
    "more_or_better_data": {"answer": "no", "why": "446 unique Plasmodium peptides against NetMHCpan-4.1's >850,000 training peptides. The one genuine advantage — parasite-derived peptides from African-ancestry and Latin American donors — is too small and too noisy to exploit."},
    "confidence_score": {"answer": "no", "why": "There are seven donors and no replicate quantification scheme; the only per-row confidence is the PEAKS -10*log10(p) score, which is a search-engine score, not a measurement uncertainty."},
    "models_beatable": {"answer": "no", "why": "NetMHCpan-4.1 (2020), MHCflurry 2.0 (2020), MixMHCpred and ImmuneApp (2024) are all open, reproducible, and already benchmarked on held-out mass-spectrometry datasets with positive predictive values around 0.72-0.83."},
    "guaranteed_improvement": {"answer": "no", "why": "The first condition already fails: our model would not see everything the baseline sees, because we lack a machine-readable HLA typing table, and there is no shared evaluation split."}
  },
  "label_elsewhere": null,
