  "publication_target": null,
  "pitch": null,
  "ml_idea": "The project considered was: benchmark the open HLA class I presentation predictors (NetMHCpan-4.1, MHCflurry 2.0, MixMHCpred, ImmuneApp) on an independent, pathogen-derived immunopeptidome from seven Brazilian malaria patients, asking whether tools trained on human and tumour peptides transfer to peptides from an intracellular parasite. The question is good and would be informative either way, but this dataset cannot support it: there is no HLA allele table outside a figure, no negatives, and the length distribution of the dataset's own human control peptides is not that of a real HLA class I immunopeptidome.",
  "effort": {"hours_to_feature_table": null, "working_set_gb": 0.001, "needs_gpu": false, "peak_ram_gb": 1, "embedding_hours": 0.02, "cv_run_minutes": null, "compute_note": "Estimated, and partly timed: all usable files total under 1 MB and parsed in seconds on this laptop; embedding 446 short peptides with a small ESM-2 checkpoint on CPU would take about a minute. Compute was never the constraint here.", "cheapest_killing_check": "Plot the peptide length distribution of the dataset's own human peptides. It took two minutes and killed the project: a real anti-HLA class I pulldown gives a strong nine-mer peak, and these give 14% nine-mers spread flat out to 25 residues."},
  "verdict": "reject",
  "verdict_reason": "Fails A2, A5 and A8, all verified in the files rather than inferred from the paper. A2: no label column exists anywhere — presentation has positives only, T cell recognition lives in figure panels, and HLA allele assignment would need deconvolution. A5: 446 unique Plasmodium peptides from 7 donors, with the measured immunogenicity label covering just 50 peptides. A8: the only written-down binary label ('binder'/'non-binder') is NetMHCpan 4.0 output, so training on it distils an existing model. On top of that the presentation label itself looks unreliable: PEAKS 7 at -10*log10(p) >= 15 with no stated false-discovery rate, 43% of rows below 20, keratin contaminants among the top human hits, 43 of the 50 synthesised peptides sharing measurable identity with the orthologous human protein, and — the decisive check — a flat 8-to-25-residue length distribution in both the parasite and the human peptide sets instead of the nine-mer-dominated profile a genuine HLA class I immunopeptidome shows. The biology is important and the Nature claims may well be right on other evidence, but as an ML substrate this is a positives-only list of a few hundred sequences whose labels I cannot trust."
}
